{"seconds": 85.3, "exit": 0, "final": "So White wins the rook! But actually, let me verify the main line I found is really mate. Let me double check:\n\n1. Bxg6+ Kxg6\n2. f5+ Kh7\n3. g6+ Kh6\n4. Rh8# \n\nWait, after g6+ the king is on h6, not h7. Let me verify this is checkmate. The king on h6 has no escape squares because:\n- g7 is controlled by the pawn on g6\n- h7 is controlled by the rook on h8\n- g6 is occupied by the pawn\n- g5 is controlled by the pawn on g5\n- h5 is occupied by the black pawn\n\nSo yes, Rh8 is checkmate!\n\nSo the best move is **Bxg6+** and the main line leads to forced mate in 4.\n\n---\n\n**Best move:** Bxg6+\n\n**Main line:** Bxg6+ Kxg6 f5+ Kh7 g6+ Kh6 Rh8#\n\n**Motif:** fork (the check forks king and pawn) leading to a mating attack\n\n**Coaching note:**\n\nWhen you see a forcing check that also threatens material (here, Bxg6+ wins the g6 pawn with check), calculate the king's flight squares carefully. Black's king gets pushed to the edge: f5+ drives it to h7, then g6+ to h6, trapping it for back-rank mate. The key insight is that each forcing move\u2014check after check\u2014gives Black no time to create counterplay. Look for checks that restrict the opponent's king movement.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Bxg6+\", \"line\": [\"Bxg6+\", \"Kxg6\", \"f5+\", \"Kh7\", \"g6+\", \"Kh6\", \"Rh8#\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\", \"attraction\", \"backRankMate\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "2R5/1p1r1kp1/1P2p1p1/p2p2Pp/3P1P1P/1K1Bb3/8/8 w - - 0 42"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "2R5/1p1r1kp1/1P2p1p1/p2p2Pp/3P1P1P/1K1Bb3/8/8 w - - 0 42"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "2R5/1p1r1kp1/1P2p1p1/p2p2Pp/3P1P1P/1K1Bb3/8/8 w - - 0 42"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "2R5/1p1r1kp1/1P2p1p1/p2p2Pp/3P1P1P/1K1Bb3/8/8 w - - 0 42", "move": "Rf8+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "5R2/1p1r1kp1/1P2p